# .dockerignore — what gets sent to the Docker daemon as build context.
#
# DENY EVERYTHING, THEN RE-ADMIT. This file is an allowlist, not a denylist, and that
# is a deliberate security choice rather than a style one. A denylist has to predict
# every future file worth excluding; the day someone adds `.env.staging` or dumps a
# 2 GB checkpoint into the repo root, a denylist silently ships it. The repo root here
# already contains `.env` (a live Anthropic key), `venv/` (hundreds of MB), `models/`
# and `adapters/` (~1.7 GB of weights) — and the image is built from a PUBLIC repo.
#
# Docker does NOT read .gitignore. Being gitignored protects nothing here.
#
# PSEUDOCODE
#     1. `*` excludes every top-level entry.
#     2. `!<path>` re-admits exactly the four things the image needs.
#     3. Re-deny build noise that lives inside those admitted directories.

*

# --- the only things the image is allowed to see ------------------------------
# pyproject.toml   the dependency set and the wheel build config
# src/             the package — including src/guardrail/dataset/data/, which is why
#                  all 662 corpus prompts ship in the wheel with no data volume
# scripts/         gate.py / run_eval.py — the CLI entrypoints CI drives
# benchmarks/      baselines.json (MEASURED) + gate_policy.json (CHOSEN)
# tests/           only reaches the `test` stage; the runtime stage never copies it
!pyproject.toml
!src
!scripts
!benchmarks
!tests

# --- re-deny noise inside the admitted paths ----------------------------------
# Stale host bytecode would be copied over the fresh source and can shadow it.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/.DS_Store
